  end on

define phrase clothes-daydream
  These are the sorts of costumes that you imagine while lying on your straw
  mattress back at Maiden House, dreaming about dazzling the Prince at the
  Royal Ball. Stupid fantasies, you know, but oh — if you could only wear
  something as beautiful as these, just for one evening...
end phrase

define phrase clothes-flinch
  Suddenly, you remember that you're supposed to be looking at the men's
  clothing. You glance nervously at the shopkeeper, but he doesn't seem to
  find your intense interest in ladies' formal wear strange in the slightest.
end phrase

define trait buy-from-clothier
  on the player buying
    refuse clothier-wont-sell
  end on
end trait

define phrase clothier-wont-sell
  The clothier is not likely to sell anything to a dirty urchin such as
  yourself. Besides, you're only interested in the dresses, and it would raise
  awkward questions if you asked him to sell you one.
end phrase

create the clothier
  aka tailor, proprietor, shopkeeper, shop keeper, merchant, man, thin man
  a person
  pronouns he
  in the Royal Tunic

  Tall, thin, and impeccably dressed, although his fashion sense seems a
  bit...decorative, compared to the more austere look preferred by men of
  Toresal's upper class. His hairline is receding, and what hair he has left is
  plastered back onto his scalp with fragrant oil. He looks as though he'd
  secretly prefer to wipe things down with a scented handkerchief before
  touching them; somehow, through good breeding and intestinal fortitude, he
  manages to soldier on despite not doing so.

  phrase present:
    The clothier stands nearby, eyeing you with some distaste.

  on every turn while the clothier is here and one chance in 5
    phrase clothier-idle
  end on

  on the player talking
    refuse clothier-sniffs
  end on

define phrase clothier-idle, randomly
  The clothier absentmindedly flicks a bit of lint off of his sleeve.
or
  The clothier wanders over to one of the dresses on display and fusses with
  the sleeve.
or
  The clothier wanders over to one of the dresses on display and fusses with
  the hem.
or
  The clothier wanders over to one of the dresses on display and fusses with
  the neckline.
or
  The clothier inspects his fingernails.
or
  The clothier purses his lips and hums something indecipherable.
or
  The clothier slowly looks you up and down. You get the distinct and
  not-exactly comfortable feeling that he's 'dressing' you with his eyes.
end phrase

## `story.ni:5107` — the dirty-Jack talk refusal; `CL1` is clean-Jack's, a
## later chapter's. The clothier does not perceive Jack.

define phrase clothier-sniffs
  The clothier sniffs and pretends not to hear you.
end phrase

## `story.ni:5065`, the dirty-Jack bell paragraph, once; and `5078` on leaving.

define phrase clothier-bell
  A small bell above the door tinkles as you enter, and a man comes rushing
  out of the store's back room with a measuring cord in his hands, held up as
  though ready to begin fitting you immediately. When he sees that you're
  merely a boy (and by all appearances, not one with much in the way of
  discretionary income at that), he stops in his tracks and his expression
  grows distinctly pouty. He manages to keep most of the disappointment out of
  his voice as he asks, 'May I help you?'
end phrase

define phrase clothier-come-back
  'Come back soon, sweetie,' calls the clothier as you leave.
end phrase

## --- Sandler and Sons: Dame Sandler and Pieter (`story.ni:5212-5290`) -------
## Dame Sandler knows everything (change document; her tree is the §3e
## lineage rewrite's). `DS1` opens on the first entry, `DS14` stops the first
## exit, `DS19` answers `talk to` while Jack is dirty. The stub for DS14
## follows the source's path — the conversation happens and she may then
## leave — as one turn: the TODO prints and the walk proceeds (flagged: the
## source holds her in the shop for the conversation's length).

create Dame Sandler
  aka sandler, dame, mrs sandler, mrs. sandler, woman, proprietor, shopkeeper, shop keeper, merchant
  a person, proper
  pronouns she
  in the Jeweler's Shop

  Though in her elder years, Dame Sandler is still a tall and striking woman.
  Her long, silver hair is pulled back in a complicated bun, and she has a
  habit of inspecting her merchandise through a monocle on the end of a long,
  slender, gold-filigreed handle. Sometimes you wonder what she's doing running
  a shop (even such a relatively high-class one as this); her demeanor is more
  that of an aristocrat, if not full royalty.

  after the player examining
    first time
      phrase sandler-liking
  end after

  phrase present:
    Dame Sandler stands near the central display case. A small, dignified
    smile plays on her lips.

  on the player talking
    refuse sandler-ds19-stub
  end on

create Pieter
  aka guard, the guard, man, bodyguard
  a person, proper
  pronouns he
  in the Jeweler's Shop

  Pieter is loyal to Dame Sandler and tirelessly vigilant. You've traded
  friendly words with him in the past, but most of the time he stays quiet to
  preserve a sense of professionalism. He gives you a slight smile and nod when
  you catch his eye.

  phrase present:
    Pieter, the guard, stands discreetly near the back of the room.

  on the player talking
    refuse pieter-on-duty
  end on

define phrase sandler-liking
  Although you almost never come to her shop (everything in here is too
  expensive to buy and too well-guarded to nick), for some reason Dame Sandler
  has always taken a liking to you, and never runs you out when you visit, even
  when she has important customers to tend to. It's more of a bemused curiosity
  than real affection, but feels good to be on a friendly basis with such an
  intriguing woman.
end phrase

define phrase pieter-on-duty
  'I can't really talk while I'm on duty, Jack,' says Pieter. 'Maybe later.'
end phrase

